{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
*Context*

Please refer to [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/RFE-818|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/RFE-818|smart-link] and [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/OAT/pages/2170093587/RFE-818+QTI+3.x+support|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/OAT/pages/2170093587/RFE-818+QTI+3.x+support|smart-link] for context.

The goal of this RFE is to prepare for and obtain the QTI 3 Entry Level certification: Import, author, deliver, and export QTI 3 packages with basic features.

(!) {color:#ff5630}*In this phase, QTI 3 support is limited to a few features that already exist in QTI 2.2.*{color}
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
This Epic is a follow up to [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/PCR-12|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/PCR-12|smart-link] as some new gaps have been identified to reach the certification. These gaps are listed under some of the child tasks, such as:

* [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4473|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4473|smart-link] 
* [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4474|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4474|smart-link] 
* [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4475|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4475|smart-link] 
* [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4704|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4704|smart-link] 
* [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4705|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4705|smart-link] 
* [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4209|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/browse/AUT-4209|smart-link] 
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance Criteria:*

# TAO Authoring (3.x/Construct) can import a QTI3 package *with the support of QTI 2.2 features only*
# TAO Authoring (3.x/Construct) can export a QTI 3 package with all the features currently supported.
# TAO is successfully certified for QTI 3 Entry Level
## Solve the gaps documented the subtasks mentioned above
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*How/what to test*

* The QTI 3 certification process is similar to the QTI 2.2 certification process explained here: [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMS/pages/1737458071/QTI+2.2+Certification+Application|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMS/pages/1737458071/QTI+2.2+Certification+Application|smart-link] 
* The certification content is gathered in this page: [https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMS/pages/2853568523/QTI+3+Basic+Level+Conformance+Test+Suite|https://oat-sa.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/IMS/pages/2853568523/QTI+3+Basic+Level+Conformance+Test+Suite|smart-link] In particular, look at the zip file for the test samples and the Excel sheets for what to test.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Currently, TAO’s Test Assembly is using a very old and rudimentary implementation of displaying and searching items to be added to the test. It has a rudimentary mechanism to search for metadata, which is not properly displaying all metadata and is difficult to use overall.
{panel}

We should consider leveraging the Advanced Search for finding items and filtering by the metadata.

We can also alternatively think of a mechanism to search for items using the Advanced Search, or even browse them, then tag / select them, then pasting or dropping them in the test.

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*AS A* TAO Administrator or System Manager,

*I WANT* to easily find items in the item bank using existing metadata

*SO THAT* I can make sure that the test content accurately represents the assessment needs and requirements
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance Criteria:*

# TBD
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
Currently, TAO’s Test Assembly is using a very rudimentary way of reordering items within a section, because they must be moved *one-by-one,* and *new items are always appended at the end of a section*. More so, there is no possibility to move an item from one section to another, within the same test part or to another test part.

*Why it matters:* high-frequency author workflow pain; decreases assembly time and raises the risk of ordering mistakes in long assessments.

*Goal*: A more efficient assembly experience should support drag-and-drop reordering and, where possible, the ability to move more than one item at the same time. This would reduce manual effort, decrease assembly time, and lower the risk of ordering mistakes.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*AS A* TAO author,
*I WANT* to be able to reorder items more efficiently, through drag-and-drop, and insert new items directly where needed in a test assembly

*SO THAT* I can build and maintain large tests more quickly, with less manual work and fewer ordering errors.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance Criteria:*

# The author should be able to move items to any position in the test, through a direct operation, preferably through drag-and-drop {color:#FF991F}*[ UX NEEDED ]*{color}
!move-items.png|width=445,alt="move-items.png"!
# The author should be able to select & move multiple items at once from/to any position in the test, through a direct operation, preferably through drag-and-drop {color:#FF991F}*[ UX NEEDED ]*{color}
# The author should be able to add new items, to any position within the test {color:#FF991F}*[ UX NEEDED ]*{color}
# The author should be able to move items within the same section, or between sections, within the same test-part or to another test-part
# the author should be able to move an entire section or test-part to a different location in the test, through the same mechanism
## all the content underneath those moved sections / test-parts should move together with their parent.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
 {color:#FF5630}*[ TO BE VALIDATED ]*{color} 
Improvement of layout configuration so that authors can manage the *width of columns* in a more granular way, instead of relying only on the fixed column positions currently available in TAO.
At the moment, TAO offers column-based layout management with fixed positions. This limits the author’s ability to fine-tune the distribution of space between columns and makes it difficult to adapt the layout precisely to specific content needs.

*Why it matters*
Improves layout flexibility; supports more precise control of item structure; reduces limitations caused by fixed column configurations.

*Requirement*
TAO should allow authors to configure column widths more flexibly and precisely, so that layouts can be adapted to the specific needs of the item rather than being constrained by fixed column positions.

This feature would extend the current layout capabilities by supporting more granular control of column widths during authoring. The improvement should enable authors to better distribute space between columns and create layouts that more accurately reflect the intended design and content structure of the item.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*As a* TAO author*,*

*I want* to be able to manage column widths in a more granular way

*so that* I can create layouts that better fit my content and avoid being constrained by fixed column positions.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance criteria* 

GIVEN an author is working in an area where column-based layout is available,
WHEN the author configures the layout,
THEN the column widths can be managed in a more granular way than with the current fixed-position behavior

GIVEN an author uses a layout with configured column widths,
WHEN the content is saved, reopened, previewed, and delivered at runtime,
THEN the configured column widths are preserved and rendered in authoring and at runtime in  *TAO Advance*
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*Technical considerations*

This request *_requires a preliminary feasibility analysis_*.

The analysis should determine whether more granular or continuous column width management can be supported within the current architecture, and then *_provide an estimation based on the implementation options identified_*.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
 {color:#FF5630}*[ TO BE VALIDATED ]*{color}  
Introduce the ability to resize and position *hotspots* and *drop zones* in *Graphic Interactions* by entering exact numeric values, such as *Left*, *Top*, *Width*, and *Height*, instead of relying only on manual drag-and-resize handles.

Currently, resizing and positioning of hotspots and drop zones are managed through graphical resize points only, which makes precise layout adjustments more difficult and less consistent, especially when exact placement is required.

*Why it matters*
Improves precision during authoring, makes layout adjustments more efficient, and supports more consistent sizing and positioning of hotspots and drop zones when exact values are needed.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*As a TAO author,*

*I want* to be able to resize and position hotspots and drop zones by entering exact numeric values

*so that* I can create more precise and consistent Graphic Interactions and avoid relying only on manual drag-and-resize actions.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance criteria* 

GIVEN an author is working in a Graphic Interaction with hotspots or drop zones,
WHEN the author selects one of these elements,
THEN numeric fields for positioning and sizing are available and editable, including at least *Left*, *Top*, *Width*, and *Height*

GIVEN an author enters numeric values for *Left*, *Top*, *Width*, or *Height*,
WHEN the values are applied,
THEN the selected hotspot or drop zone is updated accordingly in the editor

GIVEN an author configures hotspots or drop zones using numeric values,
WHEN the content is saved, reopened, previewed, and delivered at runtime,
THEN the configured size and position are preserved and rendered consistently in authoring and at runtime in  *TAO Advance*
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*Technical considerations*
Technical validation is required before estimation, including verification of which Graphic Interactions can support numeric positioning and sizing consistently.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
 {color:#FF5630}*[ TO BE VALIDATED ]*{color} 
Improvement of text editing capabilities to prevent selected inline expressions or compact text strings from being split across two lines when space is limited.

Currently, some compact expressions or short inline strings may wrap in a non-desired way, even when they should remain visually grouped. This can affect not only coordinate expressions such as *A(-1;2)*, but also other compact notations or text fragments that authors may want to preserve on a single line. Since LaTeX is not used for these cases because of text-to-speech constraints, authors need another way to preserve the visual integrity of selected inline content.

*Why it matters*
Improves readability and formatting consistency; avoids unwanted line breaks in inline expressions or short text strings that should remain visually grouped.

*Requirement*
TAO should provide a way in authoring to keep selected inline expressions or compact text strings on a single line, so that they are not split unexpectedly when space is limited.

This feature would extend the current text editing capabilities by allowing authors to preserve the visual grouping of specific inline content without relying on LaTeX. It should support cases where a short block of text, notation, or mathematical expression needs to remain intact for readability and presentation purposes, while remaining compatible with text-to-speech requirements.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*As a* TAO author*,*

*I want* to be able to prevent selected inline expressions or text strings from breaking across two lines

*so that* I can preserve readability, keep important content visually consistent, and avoid unwanted line wrapping in content.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance criteria* 

GIVEN an author is working in an area where CKEditor is available,
WHEN the author applies the supported no-break behavior to a selected inline expression or text string,
THEN the selected content remains on a single line in the editor

GIVEN an author uses inline content with no-break behavior applied,
WHEN the content is saved, reopened, previewed, and delivered at runtime,
THEN the selected content remains visually grouped and is not split across two lines in authoring or at runtime in *TAO Advance.*
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*Possible implementation*
A possible implementation could be based on applying a dedicated no-break behavior to selected inline content, for example, through a wrapper element and related styling rules.

Example:
*HTML*

{noformat}<span class="no-break">A(-1;2)</span>{noformat}



CSS

{noformat}.no-break {
  white-space: nowrap !important;
  word-break: keep-all !important;
  overflow-wrap: normal !important;
  word-wrap: normal !important;
  hyphens: none !important;
}{noformat}



*Technical considerations*
This request requires deeper technical validation before estimation.

The analysis should identify the most appropriate solution to support this behavior in authoring and runtime (beyond the *Possible implementation*)



*UX consideration*

UX validation is needed to determine how the feature can be exposed to authors in a usable way.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
{color:#36B37E}*[ PRODUCT FEATURE ]*{color} {color:#FF5630}*[ TAO3.X ]*{color}  {color:#FF5630}*[ TAO ADVANCE(NEXTGEN) ]*{color} 

Improvement of authoring capabilities so that the author can define how the *student’s answer text* is aligned inside the *Text Entry* field, for example *left-aligned, centered, or right-aligned*

!image-20260702-081131.png|width=618,alt="image-20260702-081131.png"!



*Requirement*
TAO should allow the author to configure the alignment of the text that the student will enter inside the Text Entry field.

This feature would extend the current editing capabilities by allowing the author, at item creation time, to decide whether the student’s response should appear left-aligned, centered, or right-aligned within the Text Entry box.

*Scope*

* Text Entry
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*As a TAO author,*

*I want* to be able to define the alignment of the text entered by the student inside the Text Entry field

*so that* I can control the visual presentation of the response area according to the needs of the item.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance criteria* 

GIVEN an author is configuring a *Text Entry* interaction,
WHEN the author selects the input text alignment option,
THEN the author can choose whether the student’s input will be left-aligned, centered, or right-aligned inside the response field.

GIVEN an author has configured the alignment of the student input in a *Text Entry*  interaction,
WHEN the item is saved, reopened, previewed, and delivered at runtime,
THEN the configured input alignment is preserved and applied consistently in authoring and at runtime in *TAO Advance*.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*Technical consideration*
The feature should work consistently for RTL and LTR languages.



*UX consideration (this can be done after high-level estimation)*

UX validation may be useful to determine how the feature should be exposed to authors in a usable way.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#deebff}
{color:#36B37E}*[ PRODUCT FEATURE ]*{color}  {color:#FF5630}*[ TAO3.X ]*{color}   {color:#FF5630}*[ TAO ADVANCE(NEXTGEN) ]*{color}

Improvement of TAO table formatting so that authors can decide whether table rows should use alternating background colors or not, and can configure the background of specific rows and/or cells.

Currently, table background styling does not appear to be configurable enough for the required use cases. In particular, authors may need all rows to have the same background, for example all white, and only selected rows or cells highlighted with a different color. In addition.
The current behavior differs between *Current gen* and *Next gen*.

*Why it matters*
Improves layout flexibility; supports clearer visual design of tabular content; allows authors to highlight only the relevant rows or cells instead of relying on default row styling.

*Requirement*
TAO should allow authors to configure table row and cell backgrounds more flexibly, including the possibility to disable alternating row backgrounds and apply custom background colors to selected rows and/or cells.

This feature would extend the current table formatting capabilities by giving authors more control over the visual presentation of tables. It should support use cases where all rows need the same background, alternate color for the rows , as well as cases where only one row, one column, or one or more specific cells should be highlighted.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
*As a TAO author,*

*I want* to be able to decide whether TAO tables use alternating row backgrounds and to configure the background of specific rows and/or cells

*so that* I can create tables with the visual structure and highlighting needed for my content.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#e3fcef}
*Acceptance Criteria*

GIVEN an author is working with a TAO table,
WHEN the author configures the table background behavior,
THEN the author can choose whether alternating row backgrounds are applied or not.

GIVEN an author is working with a TAO table,
WHEN the author applies a background color to a selected row, column, or cell,
THEN the selected area is displayed with the configured background color in the editor.

GIVEN an author uses a table with configured row and/or cell backgrounds,
WHEN the content is saved, reopened, previewed, and delivered at runtime,
THEN the configured table background styling is preserved and rendered consistently in authoring and at runtime in  *Next gen*.


{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*UX consideration (this can be done after high-level estimation)*

UX validation may be useful to determine how the feature should be exposed to authors in a usable way.
{panel}

{panel:bgColor=#fffae6}
*Technical requirements*

Consider applying the styles using CSS variables  Check --styleeditor
{panel}
